   "line": [
    "Bc4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays the Bowdler Attack — the bishop to c4. It's remarkably common below 1600, but genuinely not a good move against the Sicilian.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "the Bowdler Attack"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 87.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n1p3/2p5/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The first step is e6 — it blunts the bishop, leaving it biting on granite, and prepares the freeing d5 break.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "e6 blunts the c4 bishop"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 135,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n1p3/2p5/2B1P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 172.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/2n1pn2/2p5/2B1P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 2 5",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "And remember, the knight to f6 is perfectly safe here — no need to fear e5, which we'd meet with d5 or a knight jump to g4 hitting the pawn.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "...Nf6 is safe; ...e5 doesn’t scare us"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 207.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/2n1pn2/2p5/2B1P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "d3"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays the quiet d3.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 224.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/2n1pn2/2pp4/2B1P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And we reap the reward of not committing d6 early — d5 in one fell swoop, grabbing the center and solving all our opening problems at once.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "...d5 in one move solves the opening"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 240,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/2n1pn2/1Bpp4/4P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "Bb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White pins with the bishop to b5. We have a pleasant choice — force a slightly better endgame with dxe4, or simply keep developing.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 343,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp2bppp/2n1pn2/1Bpp4/4P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 2 7",
   "line": [
    "Be7"
   ],
   "spoken": "We choose the fun game — the bishop to e7. No need to force the trade with a6; White may well capture on c6 anyway.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 347.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp2bppp/2n1pn2/1BppN3/4P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 3 7",
   "line": [
    "Ne5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White pounces with the knight to e5, but it's not scary — there's no real horsepower behind the attack.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 400.5,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/ppq1bppp/2n1pn2/1BppN3/4P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 4 8",
   "line": [
    "Qc7"
   ],
   "spoken": "We defend the knight the strategic way — the queen to c7, not the bishop to d7 which would surrender the bishop pair; and if a bishop comes to f4, we unpin by shifting our bishop to d6. One habit to keep with a hanging queen: before moving it, look for an intermezzo like dxe4, and always check the opponent can't reply with a queen-check desperado.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "Qc7 keeps the bishop pair",
    "look for the intermezzo before moving a hanging queen"
   ]
